<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-09-18.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="034731"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#034731" label="[03:47:31]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">daddy: I probably could do a new tagged release this weekened if you need it</span></div><div class="line" id="034849"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#034849" label="[03:48:49]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Guest50: I actually was assuming transistor to transistor logic but odds are there are probably better options and routes forward for getting trusted root hardware.</span></div><div class="line" id="034853"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034853" label="[03:48:53]">&lt;daddy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i would appreciate it, it would help me a bunch</span></div><div class="line" id="034935"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#034935" label="[03:49:35]">&lt;daddy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stagex policy is to have as little Distrust-&quot;owned&quot; code as possible and I'm hesitant to hand-make a release just to get the submodules packed up</span></div><div class="line" id="035003"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035003" label="[03:50:03]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: well we have <a rel="nofollow" href="https://libresilicon.com/">https://libresilicon.com/</a>  which one would just need to bootstrap that process to be able to make chips to get the level required in relatively short time.</span></div><div class="line" id="035452"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035452" label="[03:54:52]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nikolar: yeah, one definitely could do a 386 or better on the 1 micrometer process they published. or with some cleverness an AMD64 chip and you could skip the floating point in hardware if one is willing to trap the floating point operations and do them in software.</span></div><div class="line" id="035621"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#035621" label="[03:56:21]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it'll be dog slow but that isn't an issue for a first generation bootstrapping chip. 100khz is powerful enough to run machines needed to make a 10Mhz chip and after that everything can go a great deal faster.</span></div><div class="line" id="090727"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#090727" label="[09:07:27]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wonder if someone has an x86_64 softcore</span></div><div class="line" id="183910"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#183910" label="[18:39:10]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; nikolar: I doubt it</span></div><div class="line" id="183931"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#183931" label="[18:39:31]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; x86_64 is quite a big thing</span></div><div class="line" id="183939"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#183939" label="[18:39:39]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sorry, doubt what</span></div><div class="line" id="183952"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#183952" label="[18:39:52]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; X86_64 softcore</span></div><div class="line" id="184004"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184004" label="[18:40:04]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh i doubt too</span></div><div class="line" id="184006"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#184006" label="[18:40:06]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; Probably hard to fit on fpga</span></div><div class="line" id="184019"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184019" label="[18:40:19]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but maybe the original x64 happened to be small enough</span></div><div class="line" id="184031"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#184031" label="[18:40:31]">&lt;nikolar&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though 64 bit softcores are just generally pretty big</span></div><div class="line" id="190756"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190756" label="[19:07:56]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well there is a 486: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://opencores.org/projects/ao486">https://opencores.org/projects/ao486</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="190807"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190807" label="[19:08:07]">&lt;oriansj&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but no AMD64 cores that I know of</span></div><br /></div></body></html>